From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 07:57:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 355/358] soc: apple: s2r: place a T8132 sleep on the SMC's
 clock

On macOS the SMC stamps the sleep it registers (CLSP) within 1 ms of the
last AP step. Ours tells the SMC at its noirq suspend, well before the
commit, so where a CLSP stamp falls against our own steps is what tells
whether the SMC took the notice or saw the SoC go down.

After the AP idle notice the SMC answers no key read, so its clock can't
be read at the commit. Instead:

- macsmc reads CLKU right before the notice, after the debug link's
  settle, between two AP counter reads (get_cycles()), and hands the
  three values to apple-s2r through apple_sleep_smc_clock_mark().
- apple-s2r's T8132 finisher reads the AP counter right before the
  commit, ahead of the last cache clean, and the report after ->enter
  prints the CLKU mark and the commit on the SMC's clock, counted from
  the middle of the mark's read (mul_u64_u32_div(), so long spans
  can't overflow).
- ->begin and ->enter forget the marks of an earlier cycle.

Nothing the sleep does changes; this adds one SMC key read before the
notice. The marks don't outlive a reset: no byte beside the PMU boot
stage is known to be free (0xf802, 0xf803 and 0xf80f are iBoot's, the
others have no named owner), so they are not written there.

tools/asahi/smc-stamp-tests.py checks the reader and the order of the
reads from the sources, builds the conversion and checks it on fixed
values over a 30-day span, and kills eleven mutations.

Assisted-by: LLM
